.TH PING 8 "2026-10-30" "Wunix" "System Administration Commands"
.SH NAME
ping \- send ICMP ECHO_REQUEST to network hosts
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBping\fR [\fB\-c\fR \fICOUNT\fR] [\fB\-s\fR \fISIZE\fR] [\fB\-W\fR \fITIMEOUT\fR] \fIDESTINATION\fR
.br
\fBping\fR [\fB\-n\fR \fICOUNT\fR] [\fB\-l\fR \fISIZE\fR] [\fB\-w\fR \fITIMEOUT\fR] [\fB\-t\fR] \fIDESTINATION\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBping\fR uses the ICMP protocol's mandatory ECHO_REQUEST datagram to elicit an ICMP ECHO_RESPONSE from a host or gateway.
.PP
Supports both Unix CLI syntax (\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-s\fR, \fB\-W\fR) and Windows syntax (\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-l\fR, \fB\-w\fR, \fB\-t\fR).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-n\fR \fICOUNT\fR
Stop after sending COUNT ECHO_REQUEST packets.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-l\fR \fISIZE\fR
Specify the number of data bytes to be sent. Default is 56 (64 with ICMP header).
.TP
\fB\-W\fR, \fB\-w\fR \fITIMEOUT\fR
Time to wait for a response, in milliseconds.
.TP
\fB\-t\fR
Ping the specified host until stopped (infinite ping).
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR, \fB/?\fR
Display help and exit.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fBip\fR(8), \fBcurl\fR(1)
